In PHP, the `...` (spread operator) after a method name creates a closure. This is the "first-class callable syntax" introduced in PHP 8.1.

So `$f = $p->g(...)` creates a closure that, when called, will invoke `$p->g()` on the specific instance `$p` that was used to create the closure.

